  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies across \neastern Idaho, though mid and high-level cloudiness is actively \nstreaming out of northern Nevada. This cloud deck will \noverspread the region over the next few hours, combining with \nhigh-elevation cumulus development to spark another round of \nisolated afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms. The \nbroad Four Corners high-pressure ridge will remain the dominant \nsynoptic feature through midweek. Consequently, Wednesday and \nThursday will showcase a near-identical weather pattern driven \nby a classic diurnal cycle of afternoon instability and terrain-\nforced convection with temperatures around 90 in the lower\nelevations.\n\nA pattern shift begins late Thursday night into Friday as an \nupper-level trough moves toward the lower British Columbia \ncoastline. This feature will push the Four Corners ridge \nsouthward, lowering 500 mb heights across the Intermountain \nWest. Afternoon temperatures will begin their downward trend on \nFriday, falling from midweek maximums to near-seasonal averages \nin the mid-80s. As the core low-pressure system settles to our \nnorth over the weekend, valley temperatures will drop further \ninto the upper 70s to around 80\u00b0F. In addition to the cooler \ntemperatures, the tightening surface pressure gradient between \nthe building trough and southern ridge will bring increasingly \nbreezy conditions Friday through Sunday. Shower and thunderstorm\nchances will linger throughout the weekend, with model guidance\ncontinuing to highlight Saturday as the wettest day of the \nevent.\n\nGlobal model ensembles show several shortwave impulses embedded\nwithin the broader cyclonic flow swinging down from Western\nCanada through the early part of next week. This will maintain\npersistent troughing across the Pacific Northwest into the start\nof the new workweek. As a result, temperatures across eastern\nIdaho will remain at or slightly below normal as we wrap up\nAugust and transition into early September.",
